Day#12 - Passthrough Mixed Reality

Introduction--
--Passthrough Mixed Reality uses external headset cameras to display the real world inside a virtual environment, blending physical and digital objects together. 
Popular devices supporting this include the Meta Quest 3 and Apple Vision Pro.

 How It Works--
--Camera Feed: Real-time video from outward-facing sensors streams inside the displays.
--Digital Blending: Virtual screens, games, and objects appear directly in your physical room.

Types of Passthrough--
1. Black-and-White / Grayscale Passthrough-
--Primary Purpose: It was originally designed purely for safety (like setting up your Guardian boundary) rather than true mixed reality gaming.
--Visual Experience: The world looks like a grainy, night-vision security feed. It is difficult to read phone screens or see fine details.
--Famous Examples: Meta Quest 2, Valve Index, PlayStation VR2.

2. Full-Colour Passthrough-
--This type adds high-resolution RGB colour cameras alongside depth sensors to recreate a natural look.
--Primary Purpose: Built specifically for true mixed reality, letting you interact seamlessly with digital objects in your actual room.
--Visual Experience: The world looks realistic, vibrant, and geometrically accurate, making it sharp enough to read a phone or computer screen.
--Famous Examples: Meta Quest 3, Apple Vision Pro, Pico 4.

Advantages--
--Productivity: Enable users to collaborate with a remote team of co-workers or friends through virtual monitors, while accessing their physical keyboard and desk.
--Gaming: Create games that blend the excitement of a virtual world into the comfort and familiarity of the real world, like zombies hiding in your living room.
--Co-located Social Presence: Allow users to be both engaged in your virtual content and able to interact with people and pets in the same room, at the same time.

